WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.377 --> 00:00:01.682
- Torture convention?

00:00:01.682 --> 00:00:02.939
What's a torture convention?

00:00:02.939 --> 00:00:04.729
Is that like Comic-Con but for assholes?

00:00:04.729 --> 00:00:05.821
(laughing)

00:00:05.821 --> 00:00:06.654
Immigration.
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.654 --> 00:00:07.962
- Give us your tired, your poor,

00:00:07.962 --> 00:00:11.242
your huddled masses yearning
to breathe free, sometimes.

00:00:11.242 --> 00:00:12.296
- Today, we're gonna find out

00:00:12.296 --> 00:00:14.834
what does it take to get into America.

00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:17.314
Am I allowed to lie on this test?

00:00:17.314 --> 00:00:18.147
- Okay.

00:00:18.147 --> 00:00:18.983
- Both my parents are immigrants.

00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:20.389
But I have no idea what the steps are.

00:00:20.389 --> 00:00:22.542
I don't know if they had
to, like, submit head shots,

00:00:22.542 --> 00:00:24.110
or do like an audition reel.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.659
Hello, my name is Jay.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:30.991
I like freedom, rap music,
and I like cowboy boots.

00:00:30.991 --> 00:00:32.074
American now?

00:00:34.825 --> 00:00:36.141
- Well, I'm Hiroshi Motomura.

00:00:36.141 --> 00:00:38.383
I teach Law at the UCLA School of Law.

00:00:38.383 --> 00:00:40.493
- Is it easy to get into America?

00:00:40.493 --> 00:00:41.572
- Depends on who you are.

00:00:41.572 --> 00:00:43.309
So we let about a million
people in the country

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.492
legally every year.

00:00:44.492 --> 00:00:45.663
I mean, a lot of people
think it should be more,

00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:46.830
a lot of people think it should be less.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.293
- So you can't just come
because you want to.

00:00:48.293 --> 00:00:49.779
- You can't come because you want to.

00:00:49.779 --> 00:00:50.991
I mean, it's a long process,

00:00:50.991 --> 00:00:52.594
and it's longer for
some people than others.

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:53.549
Depends partly on the country

00:00:53.549 --> 00:00:54.934
you're from and your skill level.

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:57.121
- I hear a lot that the
immigration system is broken.

00:00:57.121 --> 00:00:58.107
- Well, yeah, it's broken.

00:00:58.107 --> 00:00:59.405
That's the one thing
that people agree about.

00:00:59.405 --> 00:01:00.498
- But how is it broken?

00:01:00.498 --> 00:01:01.535
- How is it broken, right?

00:01:01.535 --> 00:01:03.295
- Today, we're gonna find
out just how hard it would be

00:01:03.295 --> 00:01:05.827
to actually immigrate
and then become a citizen

00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:07.557
in the United States of America.

00:01:07.557 --> 00:01:10.344
- You have to pass a test
of history and civics.

00:01:10.344 --> 00:01:11.820
It is often said that a lot of Americans

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.887
wouldn't be able to pass
the citizenship test.

00:01:13.887 --> 00:01:14.980
- We're gonna find out today.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.299
- In Tennessee, they mostly only teach you

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:18.626
Tennessee history, and
that is super useless.

00:01:18.626 --> 00:01:21.041
- [Hiroshi] What do we call
the first 10 amendments

00:01:21.041 --> 00:01:22.411
to the Constitution?

00:01:22.411 --> 00:01:24.042
- The first 10, oh boy.

00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:25.481
- The Bill of Rights.
(bell dinging)

00:01:25.481 --> 00:01:26.472
- The Bill of Rights.
(bell dinging)

00:01:26.472 --> 00:01:28.011
- The Magna Carta.
(buzzing)

00:01:28.011 --> 00:01:30.656
It's been a long time since I did school.

00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:32.428
- [Hiroshi] Who is the
governor of your state?

00:01:32.428 --> 00:01:33.791
- Oh fuck.

00:01:33.791 --> 00:01:36.175
Who is the governor of our state?

00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:37.726
- Never heard of him.

00:01:37.726 --> 00:01:38.814
Who is he, what's he look like?

00:01:38.814 --> 00:01:41.416
Old, white?
(bell dinging)

00:01:41.416 --> 00:01:43.238
- [Hiroshi] What is the last day you can

00:01:43.238 --> 00:01:45.668
send in federal income tax forms?

00:01:45.668 --> 00:01:46.723
- Oh my God.

00:01:46.723 --> 00:01:47.697
I think I actually know this 'cause

00:01:47.697 --> 00:01:48.961
I always miss this date.

00:01:48.961 --> 00:01:51.101
- No, April 20th is 4/20.

00:01:51.101 --> 00:01:52.526
That's not the answer.
(buzzing)

00:01:52.526 --> 00:01:53.921
That's not the answer.

00:01:53.921 --> 00:01:56.209
- [Hiroshi] What did Susan B. Anthony do?

00:01:56.209 --> 00:02:00.367
- Susan B. Anthony
designed the first US flag.

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.464
(buzzing)

00:02:01.464 --> 00:02:02.297
Damn it.

00:02:02.297 --> 00:02:03.873
- She was a boss ass bitch,
wouldn't you know, man.

00:02:03.873 --> 00:02:04.779
- [Hiroshi] You passed.

00:02:04.779 --> 00:02:05.612
- Yeah.

00:02:06.884 --> 00:02:09.372
- Well, that's good news as
far as citizenship test goes.

00:02:09.372 --> 00:02:11.337
The more complicated part
of this is even getting

00:02:11.337 --> 00:02:13.806
in the group of people
that's allowed to take it.

00:02:13.806 --> 00:02:15.514
Unless you've had a Green
Card for five years.

00:02:15.514 --> 00:02:16.347
- Wait, what?

00:02:16.347 --> 00:02:17.575
- Someone could be waiting 10 or 15 years

00:02:17.575 --> 00:02:19.066
if they're waiting to get a Green Card.

00:02:19.066 --> 00:02:20.829
- Just for the Green Card.
- Just for the Green Card.

00:02:20.829 --> 00:02:22.007
(chattering)

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:23.320
- Let me explain what we're talking about

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.525
is coming to this country
as a so-called immigrant.

00:02:26.525 --> 00:02:27.358
You're not a citizen.

00:02:27.358 --> 00:02:29.125
This is about getting in and being allowed

00:02:29.125 --> 00:02:30.383
to stay indefinitely.
(bell dinging)

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:33.409
Being a citizen is a
whole other set of hoops

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:35.275
that you have to jump through.
- Hiroshi.

00:02:35.275 --> 00:02:37.034
- Who puts all these hoops in the air?

00:02:37.034 --> 00:02:39.496
Where are all these hoops
coming from, Hiroshi?

00:02:39.496 --> 00:02:41.581
Today we're gonna meet some
people who are citizens,

00:02:41.581 --> 00:02:42.856
some people who aren't citizens yet,

00:02:42.856 --> 00:02:43.985
but people who have come to America

00:02:43.985 --> 00:02:45.295
to live a better life.

00:02:45.295 --> 00:02:47.478
- My family is from Iran.

00:02:47.478 --> 00:02:49.846
They came to the United States

00:02:49.846 --> 00:02:51.615
right after the Iranian Revolution.

00:02:51.615 --> 00:02:53.365
My aunt was executed.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:57.113
My mom was the next one that
was supposed to be executed.

00:02:57.113 --> 00:02:59.143
She escaped with my other aunt.

00:02:59.143 --> 00:03:01.429
Both on horseback, going
through the mountains,

00:03:01.429 --> 00:03:03.710
just trying to find some place like home.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.083
- I moved to the US five
years ago to study college.

00:03:08.083 --> 00:03:09.651
Though I had to apply for a student visa,

00:03:09.651 --> 00:03:11.756
my hometown, Tampico,
Mexico, was undergoing

00:03:11.756 --> 00:03:13.789
lots of drug cartel violence at the time.

00:03:13.789 --> 00:03:16.387
I left both because of safety reasons,

00:03:16.387 --> 00:03:18.595
and also to give myself
more opportunities.

00:03:18.595 --> 00:03:19.720
- Now, we're gonna simulate how to get

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:20.885
in the country legally.

00:03:20.885 --> 00:03:22.498
And there are four different ways to get

00:03:22.498 --> 00:03:23.479
into this country.

00:03:23.479 --> 00:03:25.735
Who your relatives are, if
you have a job prospect,

00:03:25.735 --> 00:03:27.785
we also protect people
fleeing persecution,

00:03:27.785 --> 00:03:29.082
and there's this lottery.

00:03:29.082 --> 00:03:31.509
I've written out a few
pretty typical stories.

00:03:31.509 --> 00:03:32.743
A couple of 'em are from Iran.

00:03:32.743 --> 00:03:34.389
A couple of 'em are from Mexico.

00:03:34.389 --> 00:03:36.408
- Zach and I are going
to be here with Maral

00:03:36.408 --> 00:03:37.948
to represent Iran.

00:03:37.948 --> 00:03:38.946
And then Maral will help

00:03:38.946 --> 00:03:41.094
contextualize the Iranian perspective.

00:03:41.094 --> 00:03:42.937
- She's here to make sure
we don't fuck it all up.

00:03:42.937 --> 00:03:44.982
- It's crazy to say she's
gonna get in the country.

00:03:44.982 --> 00:03:47.088
I mean, its easy if you have a PhD

00:03:47.088 --> 00:03:48.577
and you've won a Nobel Prize.

00:03:48.577 --> 00:03:51.414
Eugene's this 23 year old woman,

00:03:51.414 --> 00:03:52.690
and your name is Avideh.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.677
And you're a student at a university,

00:03:54.677 --> 00:03:57.190
studying physics and chemical engineering.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.802
- Fake Iranian me is so
much smarter than real me.

00:03:59.802 --> 00:04:00.635
(laughing)

00:04:00.635 --> 00:04:02.149
- [Hiroshi] You've recently spoken out

00:04:02.149 --> 00:04:04.116
against Islamic fundamentalism.

00:04:04.116 --> 00:04:07.300
- I think I have a
crush on Iranian Eugene.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.862
- Don't have a crush on fake me.

00:04:08.862 --> 00:04:10.596
- [Hiroshi] You've received
some threatening phone calls,

00:04:10.596 --> 00:04:12.588
accusing you of subversive politics.

00:04:12.588 --> 00:04:13.849
But you don't know who made the calls.

00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:14.712
You think it would be a good idea

00:04:14.712 --> 00:04:15.996
to think about leaving Iran.

00:04:15.996 --> 00:04:17.887
And you think about the United States.

00:04:17.887 --> 00:04:20.273
- People are calling and
threatening my beautiful Avideh.

00:04:20.273 --> 00:04:21.645
- I don't know you.

00:04:21.645 --> 00:04:23.198
We've never met.

00:04:23.198 --> 00:04:25.500
- [Hiroshi] So Ned, here's your story.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.077
Now, you're a college grad, and you work

00:04:27.077 --> 00:04:29.675
as a bank officer at a
private bank in Mexico City.

00:04:29.675 --> 00:04:31.720
You often travel internationally,

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.066
and recently met and fell in
love with a man from Peru.

00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:36.891
And he got his Green Card six years ago.

00:04:36.891 --> 00:04:38.101
The two of you want to marry.

00:04:38.101 --> 00:04:40.241
- Wow, my husband.

00:04:40.241 --> 00:04:41.706
- [Hiroshi] You want to bring with you,

00:04:41.706 --> 00:04:44.711
a six year old daughter
and a four year old son

00:04:44.711 --> 00:04:46.009
from a previous marriage.

00:04:46.009 --> 00:04:48.962
- And your first husband
turned into a cat.

00:04:48.962 --> 00:04:49.995
- [Hiroshi] And you have a mother who's

00:04:49.995 --> 00:04:51.818
attached to her grandchildren,

00:04:51.818 --> 00:04:54.477
and would be heartbroken if
they went with you to LA.

00:04:54.477 --> 00:04:56.081
- Abuela, please.

00:04:56.081 --> 00:04:57.312
- Abuela.

00:04:57.312 --> 00:04:58.722
- [Hiroshi] You're a 55 year old man.

00:04:58.722 --> 00:05:00.070
(laughing)
- [Maral] What's his name?

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.058
- [Hiroshi] That's Firouz.

00:05:01.058 --> 00:05:03.025
- Oh.
- Firouz, she loves it.

00:05:03.025 --> 00:05:04.680
- I love Firouz, it's very poetic.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:05.609
- I'm poetic.

00:05:05.609 --> 00:05:06.991
- [Hiroshi] So you've
been working as a barber,

00:05:06.991 --> 00:05:08.555
living in a Tehran suburb.

00:05:08.555 --> 00:05:10.650
And I'm afraid you've
been recently widowed.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:11.864
- That means I'm single.

00:05:11.864 --> 00:05:12.697
- [Hiroshi] You have a sister,

00:05:12.697 --> 00:05:14.494
and the sister immigrated 15 years ago

00:05:14.494 --> 00:05:16.915
to Los Angeles, where
she owns a hair salon.

00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:19.568
- So I need to get my
sister to become a citizen.

00:05:19.568 --> 00:05:21.195
- And then you could
just marry your sister.

00:05:21.195 --> 00:05:22.134
- No.

00:05:22.134 --> 00:05:24.541
No, Maral, that's not how that works.

00:05:24.541 --> 00:05:26.088
- [Hiroshi] Keith, you work as a carpenter

00:05:26.088 --> 00:05:28.004
and a landscaper in Mexico.

00:05:28.004 --> 00:05:29.156
The whole construction industry

00:05:29.156 --> 00:05:31.823
has been drying up in your town.

00:05:33.191 --> 00:05:34.527
- [Hiroshi] You think your prospects,

00:05:34.527 --> 00:05:36.901
who are your three children,
would be much better

00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:38.164
in the United States.

00:05:38.164 --> 00:05:39.197
- I'm a family man.

00:05:39.197 --> 00:05:40.434
I wanna set a good example.

00:05:40.434 --> 00:05:41.466
- [Hiroshi] One thing I didn't tell you,

00:05:41.466 --> 00:05:42.862
that you dropped out of high school.

00:05:42.862 --> 00:05:44.108
- Fuck.

00:05:44.108 --> 00:05:44.961
Shouldn't have done that.

00:05:44.961 --> 00:05:47.054
But times were different, I
was partying a lot back then.

00:05:47.054 --> 00:05:47.981
(dance music)

00:05:47.981 --> 00:05:50.402
- Or you couldn't afford to
be in school, realistically.

00:05:50.402 --> 00:05:51.469
You just need to work.

00:05:51.469 --> 00:05:52.873
- There's a lot of other reasons

00:05:52.873 --> 00:05:55.870
you might drop out from high
school other than partying.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:57.449
- I like to party.
(dance music)

00:05:57.449 --> 00:05:58.697
I'm not saying everybody likes to party.

00:05:58.697 --> 00:06:00.219
I'm just me, I'm me.

00:06:00.219 --> 00:06:01.519
Mariano, party man.

00:06:01.519 --> 00:06:02.866
(bright music)
- [Hiroshi] Okay, so,

00:06:02.866 --> 00:06:04.100
you've got a tough choice to make.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.121
- Wow, this is a lot to un-package.

00:06:06.121 --> 00:06:08.635
- Do I have any super powers?

00:06:08.635 --> 00:06:10.613
- What do we know about Avideh?

00:06:10.613 --> 00:06:12.123
- So I would say that she comes from

00:06:12.123 --> 00:06:13.597
a pretty well-off family.

00:06:13.597 --> 00:06:15.308
And also that she's highly intelligent.

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:17.178
- And like, naturally beautiful.

00:06:17.178 --> 00:06:19.295
- Just like, with no makeup whatsoever.

00:06:19.295 --> 00:06:20.409
- He works at a bank.

00:06:20.409 --> 00:06:21.740
- You have some funds.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:22.596
You have an income.

00:06:22.596 --> 00:06:23.429
- Rob the bank.

00:06:23.429 --> 00:06:24.490
- No, I'm not gonna rob the bank.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:25.323
- Rob the bank.

00:06:25.323 --> 00:06:26.360
- But if you don't have family here,

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:27.916
you don't have a job,
you don't have a claim

00:06:27.916 --> 00:06:31.076
for persecution, and you don't
win the lottery, you're out.

00:06:31.076 --> 00:06:32.151
- I'm going to go for a work visa

00:06:32.151 --> 00:06:34.578
because I have the most magical
fingers in all of Tehran.

00:06:34.578 --> 00:06:36.648
- [Hiroshi] If it's possible,
your sister could sponsor you.

00:06:36.648 --> 00:06:38.189
But she's gonna have to show that

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:43.134
you are not taking a job
away from an American worker.

00:06:43.134 --> 00:06:44.389
- That's the one I had to fill out.

00:06:44.389 --> 00:06:45.666
- You had to prove that you wouldn't take

00:06:45.666 --> 00:06:46.638
anyone else's jobs?

00:06:46.638 --> 00:06:47.471
- [Andrea] Yeah.

00:06:47.471 --> 00:06:49.003
- [Hiroshi] They have to
basically advertise the job.

00:06:49.003 --> 00:06:51.975
If everyone was rejected
for a legitimate reason,

00:06:51.975 --> 00:06:53.576
then you're the only
one left standing there,

00:06:53.576 --> 00:06:54.619
we can hire you, but then that

00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:56.288
process can take several years.

00:06:56.288 --> 00:06:57.148
- Do we have scissors here?

00:06:57.148 --> 00:06:59.102
'Cause I can show you how good I am.

00:06:59.102 --> 00:07:01.354
I think like a nice fade on the side.

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:03.864
- Stop making us not
want Firouz to immigrate.

00:07:03.864 --> 00:07:05.245
You're already insufferable.

00:07:05.245 --> 00:07:08.098
- I have got some dependents.

00:07:08.098 --> 00:07:10.612
Am I allowed to go to LA
just to like, to visit?

00:07:10.612 --> 00:07:12.908
- Tourist visa's a so-called
non-immigrant visa.

00:07:12.908 --> 00:07:14.759
That means that you have
to convince someone,

00:07:14.759 --> 00:07:16.491
let's say at the US embassy,

00:07:16.491 --> 00:07:18.944
that if you come in to the
United States as a tourist,

00:07:18.944 --> 00:07:20.477
you will actually leave.

00:07:20.477 --> 00:07:22.680
- So, no ties in the US.
- I can overstay

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:23.513
on my tourist visa.

00:07:23.513 --> 00:07:25.529
Oh, okay, so, I was gonna say,

00:07:25.529 --> 00:07:27.331
get the tourist visa, go get married.

00:07:27.331 --> 00:07:29.254
- So the US government
isn't gonna give you a visa

00:07:29.254 --> 00:07:30.994
if they think you might overstay.

00:07:30.994 --> 00:07:33.103
- Love knows no laws.

00:07:33.103 --> 00:07:34.432
Love knows no laws.
- Then rob the bank.

00:07:34.432 --> 00:07:37.915
- Well I might have well
founded persecution, right?

00:07:37.915 --> 00:07:38.838
- [Hiroshi] You're gonna have to show

00:07:38.838 --> 00:07:40.381
that this amounts to some kind of pattern

00:07:40.381 --> 00:07:42.012
where you're really under
some kind of threat.

00:07:42.012 --> 00:07:43.092
- You don't think that his threatening

00:07:43.092 --> 00:07:44.517
phone calls are credible enough?

00:07:44.517 --> 00:07:45.835
- [Hiroshi] You've only
gotten a few calls.

00:07:45.835 --> 00:07:46.735
- [Zach] Hiroshi, you say a few

00:07:46.735 --> 00:07:48.359
as if this is a normal thing.

00:07:48.359 --> 00:07:50.615
- [Hiroshi] You don't exactly
wanna put yourself out there

00:07:50.615 --> 00:07:52.356
until you get more threats, right?

00:07:52.356 --> 00:07:53.738
- There are so many countries in the world

00:07:53.738 --> 00:07:55.634
that you say one thing, and it's a life

00:07:55.634 --> 00:07:57.547
and death situation, and you're done.

00:07:57.547 --> 00:07:59.187
- I'd much rather have a student visa

00:07:59.187 --> 00:08:01.214
than put myself in more danger

00:08:01.214 --> 00:08:02.645
to prove that I'm in danger.

00:08:02.645 --> 00:08:03.812
- [Hiroshi] Turns out you have a cousin

00:08:03.812 --> 00:08:06.859
who's living, but without
papers, in Los Angeles,

00:08:06.859 --> 00:08:08.612
and he offers to do what he can

00:08:08.612 --> 00:08:10.206
to help you move to LA.

00:08:10.206 --> 00:08:13.146
- [Ned] And is that a quick,
quick application process?

00:08:13.146 --> 00:08:14.699
- [Hiroshi] There is a
temporary worker program.

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:16.643
- Like a five year visa?

00:08:16.643 --> 00:08:18.756
- I think in terms
practically speaking, a year.

00:08:18.756 --> 00:08:19.713
- A year?

00:08:19.713 --> 00:08:20.546
- [Hiroshi] You might be thinking,

00:08:20.546 --> 00:08:21.650
I wanna get a Green Card.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:22.852
The problem is it's really hard to do that

00:08:22.852 --> 00:08:24.234
if you don't have a college degree.

00:08:24.234 --> 00:08:26.438
- Could I finish high school now,

00:08:26.438 --> 00:08:28.642
and apply to a college?

00:08:28.642 --> 00:08:29.688
- That's an option.

00:08:29.688 --> 00:08:30.543
- [Keith] But I need to make money.

00:08:30.543 --> 00:08:32.098
I don't need to pay money.

00:08:32.098 --> 00:08:33.279
- [Andrea] You have a family.

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:35.931
- Do I have any friends with
strong Photoshop skills,

00:08:35.931 --> 00:08:37.716
and I could get a diploma?

00:08:37.716 --> 00:08:38.961
But it's a fake diploma?

00:08:38.961 --> 00:08:40.590
- So you're relying on family connection.

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:42.045
- I think that's all I got.

00:08:42.045 --> 00:08:43.705
- [Hiroshi] Your sister
could become a citizen,

00:08:43.705 --> 00:08:46.020
if she isn't already,
and she can sponsor you,

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.010
but the problem with that is

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.514
then you get into a line
that's many years long.

00:08:49.514 --> 00:08:50.932
- Is that not the fastest line?

00:08:50.932 --> 00:08:52.358
- Could be 15 or 20 years.

00:08:52.358 --> 00:08:53.484
- 15 to 20 years?

00:08:53.484 --> 00:08:55.027
- [Hiroshi] Depends partly
on the country you're from.

00:08:55.027 --> 00:08:56.240
- [Keith] That's a long line.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.351
- [Hiroshi] For some people,
it's a retirement program.

00:08:58.351 --> 00:09:00.473
- God, I need to, I'm
gonna be here forever.

00:09:00.473 --> 00:09:02.024
- Every month, the state
department puts out

00:09:02.024 --> 00:09:04.070
this list of who we're letting in today.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.553
So if you're from a big country,

00:09:05.553 --> 00:09:07.292
in terms of population,
you gotta wait longer

00:09:07.292 --> 00:09:09.126
than if you're from a smaller country.

00:09:09.126 --> 00:09:12.694
But if you marry someone
who is a US citizen,

00:09:12.694 --> 00:09:14.369
then technically, there's no line.

00:09:14.369 --> 00:09:15.374
- Where should we get married,

00:09:15.374 --> 00:09:18.240
and what type of dressed should
I have the bridesmaids wear?

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.439
- [Andrea] That's the
important question here.

00:09:19.439 --> 00:09:21.116
- [Hiroshi] One thing that
does count in your favor

00:09:21.116 --> 00:09:23.545
is that you can bring
your children with you

00:09:23.545 --> 00:09:25.216
- [Both] What about abuelita?

00:09:25.216 --> 00:09:27.889
- The problem is you
can bring your children,

00:09:27.889 --> 00:09:30.081
but not your parents.

00:09:30.081 --> 00:09:31.666
- She gets left behind.

00:09:31.666 --> 00:09:32.510
- But think about this.

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:33.954
It's gonna take like 10 years.

00:09:33.954 --> 00:09:34.982
How old is abuelita?

00:09:34.982 --> 00:09:36.714
I was being honest.

00:09:36.714 --> 00:09:38.937
You know, it might not be a problem.

00:09:38.937 --> 00:09:40.209
You know, people come and go.

00:09:40.209 --> 00:09:42.018
- And I don't want a Winter wedding.

00:09:42.018 --> 00:09:42.851
- Just let me just mention

00:09:42.851 --> 00:09:45.540
a couple complications
with Avideh's scenario.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.641
- You don't have to.

00:09:46.641 --> 00:09:48.313
- [Hiroshi] One is that
you've gotta be able

00:09:48.313 --> 00:09:50.725
to prove financial
support for this period.

00:09:50.725 --> 00:09:52.174
'Cause you won't be allowed to work.

00:09:52.174 --> 00:09:53.484
- Oh my gosh, and then
you have to find a job

00:09:53.484 --> 00:09:54.585
within your field of study.

00:09:54.585 --> 00:09:56.041
So it's not like you can be a waitress

00:09:56.041 --> 00:09:57.690
for like to pay rent, or something.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:59.133
You have 90 days to find a job

00:09:59.133 --> 00:10:00.644
within your field of study.

00:10:00.644 --> 00:10:01.501
- I am not dating you.

00:10:01.501 --> 00:10:02.765
You do not have any money.

00:10:02.765 --> 00:10:04.111
We know what he was going to say.

00:10:04.111 --> 00:10:06.730
- I'm currently on an H1B work visa,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:08.641
a visa that the current administration

00:10:08.641 --> 00:10:10.567
has promised to get rid of.

00:10:10.567 --> 00:10:11.612
- If you got a permanent job where

00:10:11.612 --> 00:10:12.953
the employer could sponsor you.

00:10:12.953 --> 00:10:13.986
This is complicated, though.

00:10:13.986 --> 00:10:16.350
- My path to citizenship
would be through my employer,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.703
so I would need to stay
with my organization

00:10:18.703 --> 00:10:20.338
for a long time for them to sponsor me

00:10:20.338 --> 00:10:21.845
through the whole Green Card process.

00:10:21.845 --> 00:10:22.906
- This is the plight of all of my

00:10:22.906 --> 00:10:24.202
international student friends.

00:10:24.202 --> 00:10:26.014
- I have a couple of friends
who are international

00:10:26.014 --> 00:10:27.861
students with me, and they
weren't able to find jobs,

00:10:27.861 --> 00:10:28.999
so now they have to go home.

00:10:28.999 --> 00:10:30.594
Fortunately, that was not the case for me.

00:10:30.594 --> 00:10:31.840
- Does this mean we're getting married?

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.193
- Hell no.
- Damn.

00:10:33.193 --> 00:10:35.684
- Okay, so I feel like my plan now,

00:10:35.684 --> 00:10:37.790
since I don't wanna leave abuelita,

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:40.874
I'm gonna get Eduardo to
apply for his citizenship,

00:10:40.874 --> 00:10:44.586
get married, maybe we'll live
in Mexico City for a while,

00:10:44.586 --> 00:10:48.086
tend to abuela, and then after she passes,

00:10:49.193 --> 00:10:50.693
we can move to LA.

00:10:53.122 --> 00:10:53.955
- Ouch.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:57.540
- She's going to die at some point.

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:58.493
I don't know.

00:10:58.493 --> 00:10:59.679
I don't know what to do.

00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:00.989
- I'm gonna build a boat.

00:11:00.989 --> 00:11:02.031
- [Hiroshi] That sounds very biblical.

00:11:02.031 --> 00:11:03.279
- [Keith] And I'm gonna sell the boat.

00:11:03.279 --> 00:11:04.112
Now I got some money.

00:11:04.112 --> 00:11:05.206
I'm gonna build another boat.

00:11:05.206 --> 00:11:06.357
Put the family on that boat.

00:11:06.357 --> 00:11:07.769
We're gonna go up the coast.

00:11:07.769 --> 00:11:08.986
We're in America.

00:11:08.986 --> 00:11:10.492
- I don't know if that's how it works.

00:11:10.492 --> 00:11:11.325
- Why can't it work?

00:11:11.325 --> 00:11:13.280
Has anyone ever tried
the double boat method?

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:14.419
- He's getting creative, here.

00:11:14.419 --> 00:11:16.087
- I've gotta do something
to save my family.

00:11:16.087 --> 00:11:17.375
I got three kinds depending on me.

00:11:17.375 --> 00:11:18.924
There's no path for me.

00:11:18.924 --> 00:11:19.780
- A lot of this has to do with

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:22.241
a mis-match between what our economy

00:11:22.241 --> 00:11:24.544
seems to want, and the
people we actually let in.

00:11:24.544 --> 00:11:25.823
Millions of employers in this country

00:11:25.823 --> 00:11:28.049
who think they need immigrant workers.

00:11:28.049 --> 00:11:29.571
We have a congress that's not willing to

00:11:29.571 --> 00:11:31.691
create lines to stand in

00:11:31.691 --> 00:11:32.790
if you don't have a college degree.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:33.750
- People wanna hire me.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:34.583
- There are jobs.

00:11:34.583 --> 00:11:37.836
- I can build boats, boats,
boats, boats for days.

00:11:37.836 --> 00:11:38.669
- So a lot of people say,

00:11:38.669 --> 00:11:39.682
why don't you stand in line?

00:11:39.682 --> 00:11:41.074
Well, there's no line to stand in.

00:11:41.074 --> 00:11:42.581
But also, immigrants do a lot of the work

00:11:42.581 --> 00:11:44.449
that keeps businesses in this country,

00:11:44.449 --> 00:11:46.441
and they create jobs
for all kinds of people.

00:11:46.441 --> 00:11:48.066
Including a lot of Americans.

00:11:48.066 --> 00:11:49.404
- [Keith] What is this paperwork?

00:11:49.404 --> 00:11:50.412
- [Hiroshi] Well, the
form you're looking at

00:11:50.412 --> 00:11:52.794
is for someone who wants
to get a Green Card.

00:11:52.794 --> 00:11:56.241
- I think you gave me
multiple, by accident.

00:11:56.241 --> 00:11:57.506
- It's giving me anxiety just to see them

00:11:57.506 --> 00:12:00.302
because I've had to fill out
so many of these forms before.

00:12:00.302 --> 00:12:02.733
- There's a separate form
for temporary workers.

00:12:02.733 --> 00:12:04.803
- And then this is to become a citizen?

00:12:04.803 --> 00:12:07.150
- Five years later, you
could apply for citizenship.

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:08.160
- I'm gonna be dead by then.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.727
- Maybe I should just go to Mexico City.

00:12:09.727 --> 00:12:11.321
Seems like there's a lot of banks there.

00:12:11.321 --> 00:12:12.538
- I bet our kids would be friends.

00:12:12.538 --> 00:12:13.902
- You know, the fees could run you in

00:12:13.902 --> 00:12:16.458
the hundreds to a few
thousands of dollars.

00:12:16.458 --> 00:12:18.934
Lawyers fees might run
you thousands of dollars,

00:12:18.934 --> 00:12:20.612
if there are complicated
issues in the case.

00:12:20.612 --> 00:12:21.708
- I don't think I have enough money

00:12:21.708 --> 00:12:24.454
to fill out these forms, and eat.

00:12:24.454 --> 00:12:26.831
- But there's one other
complication I wanna mention,

00:12:26.831 --> 00:12:30.169
and that is a travel ban in effect.

00:12:30.169 --> 00:12:31.671
- Oh yeah, that thing.

00:12:31.671 --> 00:12:34.417
- [Hiroshi] The basic idea
is to suspend immigration

00:12:34.417 --> 00:12:37.683
from first seven, now
six different countries.

00:12:37.683 --> 00:12:40.016
I mean, the one that's most
affected is Iran, naturally.

00:12:40.016 --> 00:12:41.664
I mean, about half the people affected.

00:12:41.664 --> 00:12:43.717
And there's also a separate reduction

00:12:43.717 --> 00:12:45.623
number of refugees that
are allowed to come in.

00:12:45.623 --> 00:12:47.470
So it's been defended as a
national security measure,

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:49.584
but it's being challenged by saying

00:12:49.584 --> 00:12:52.202
that it's a pretext, is
also challenged by saying,

00:12:52.202 --> 00:12:54.369
hey, this is really targeting Muslims.

00:12:54.369 --> 00:12:58.068
It's following one of the
campaign promise of a Muslim ban.

00:12:58.068 --> 00:13:00.569
- I remember distinctively
the day that my mom

00:13:00.569 --> 00:13:02.589
texted me about the travel ban.

00:13:02.589 --> 00:13:04.007
And she was like, call me.

00:13:04.007 --> 00:13:07.020
Like, I could hear the tears in her voice.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:09.469
It was very much of her just talking about

00:13:09.469 --> 00:13:12.636
how many families don't have a future.

00:13:14.452 --> 00:13:15.535
- [Hiroshi] Becoming a US citizen

00:13:15.535 --> 00:13:18.437
was only available to free white persons.

00:13:18.437 --> 00:13:20.493
It's only in 1952.

00:13:20.493 --> 00:13:21.852
- 1952.

00:13:21.852 --> 00:13:24.544
- 1952 was the first time congress says

00:13:24.544 --> 00:13:26.037
we're gonna let you become a citizen

00:13:26.037 --> 00:13:28.356
regardless of country you're from.

00:13:28.356 --> 00:13:29.924
What we think it was normal America

00:13:29.924 --> 00:13:31.735
used to be seen as really exotic.

00:13:31.735 --> 00:13:33.915
But it was a time in the 1800s where

00:13:33.915 --> 00:13:36.055
the real concern was Catholics.

00:13:36.055 --> 00:13:38.533
Because they owed allegiance to the Pope,

00:13:38.533 --> 00:13:41.350
and they couldn't possibly
be good Americans.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.894
History doesn't repeat
itself, but it often rhymes.

00:13:43.894 --> 00:13:46.028
(slow music)

00:13:46.028 --> 00:13:48.324
- I chose to go a more
student based route.

00:13:48.324 --> 00:13:49.970
- Yeah, you might, and a lot of people do

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.485
leave themselves open
to getting job offers

00:13:52.485 --> 00:13:53.769
when they graduate.

00:13:53.769 --> 00:13:55.791
That is going to allow
you to get a Green Card.

00:13:55.791 --> 00:13:57.325
But there are a lot of
pitfalls along the way,

00:13:57.325 --> 00:13:58.767
and a lot of people don't make it through.

00:13:58.767 --> 00:14:01.615
I mean, a lot of people don't
ever find that employer.

00:14:01.615 --> 00:14:02.980
You don't know how long it's gonna take.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:04.974
You don't know if something will go wrong.

00:14:04.974 --> 00:14:07.983
Having your mom come visit you later,

00:14:07.983 --> 00:14:10.268
that might be tough,
because there's a suspicion

00:14:10.268 --> 00:14:11.685
maybe she'll stay.

00:14:11.685 --> 00:14:13.983
And you gotta make some
choices about your family life.

00:14:13.983 --> 00:14:16.869
- It's like I can ultimately
make it to America,

00:14:16.869 --> 00:14:19.648
but it would come at
great personal sacrifice.

00:14:19.648 --> 00:14:22.107
- Even though that's the
quickest path, supposedly.

00:14:22.107 --> 00:14:22.996
- Wow, wow.

00:14:22.996 --> 00:14:25.286
- And it's gotten so much
harder over the years.

00:14:25.286 --> 00:14:26.941
She can try to sponsor you,

00:14:26.941 --> 00:14:28.358
but that's a really long line.

00:14:28.358 --> 00:14:30.570
It's really measured in decades now.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:31.517
- [Zach] Decades.

00:14:31.517 --> 00:14:32.941
- I mean, I suppose there
are people who think

00:14:32.941 --> 00:14:34.762
that's the way the system ought to be.

00:14:34.762 --> 00:14:37.839
But no matter where you
come out on that, I mean,

00:14:37.839 --> 00:14:40.363
the fact is it's really hard
to get into this country.

00:14:40.363 --> 00:14:41.966
- How many people would you say

00:14:41.966 --> 00:14:43.476
are wanting to come to America,

00:14:43.476 --> 00:14:45.156
but there's just no path?

00:14:45.156 --> 00:14:47.054
- The answer's gotta be in the millions,

00:14:47.054 --> 00:14:48.781
because, you know, we
have 11 million people

00:14:48.781 --> 00:14:51.276
in this country who
don't have legal status,

00:14:51.276 --> 00:14:53.308
and people sometimes say, why
don't you just stand in line.

00:14:53.308 --> 00:14:54.795
Well, I'm not sure there's much of a line

00:14:54.795 --> 00:14:55.990
for you to stand in.

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:57.500
- I'm gonna come here without papers

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.858
and just send money back to
my family until they find me.

00:14:59.858 --> 00:15:01.291
- Well, a lot of people are doing that.

00:15:01.291 --> 00:15:02.124
- That's what I would do.

00:15:02.124 --> 00:15:04.579
And I totally understand
why you'd do that.

00:15:04.579 --> 00:15:07.368
- I'm ashamed at how
much I was unaware of.

00:15:07.368 --> 00:15:09.179
So much of whether or not
you can get to America

00:15:09.179 --> 00:15:10.240
is out of your control.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:11.509
It's where were you born,

00:15:11.509 --> 00:15:13.362
how much money were you born into,

00:15:13.362 --> 00:15:15.520
did you have the privilege
and the opportunity

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:16.883
to get an education?

00:15:16.883 --> 00:15:18.967
Because guess what, you
could also work really hard,

00:15:18.967 --> 00:15:21.080
and still not get it.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.138
- Many times, we talk about immigrants

00:15:22.138 --> 00:15:24.277
with this blanket terminology.

00:15:24.277 --> 00:15:26.183
When in fact, it's a very nuanced,

00:15:26.183 --> 00:15:30.009
highly personal, diverse,
perspective-driven process

00:15:30.009 --> 00:15:32.290
that changes from
individual to individual,

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:33.619
and generally, it favors people

00:15:33.619 --> 00:15:36.749
who have higher educations
and a little bit more money.

00:15:36.749 --> 00:15:38.211
And so I think that's
the sad thing is that

00:15:38.211 --> 00:15:41.361
the most un-American thing
is trying to block people out

00:15:41.361 --> 00:15:43.278
who are doing the most American thing,

00:15:43.278 --> 00:15:45.198
working hard to have a better life.

00:15:45.198 --> 00:15:46.566
- For anyone to have a perspective of

00:15:46.566 --> 00:15:48.981
those people shouldn't
be here, America first,

00:15:48.981 --> 00:15:50.155
it's garbage.

00:15:50.155 --> 00:15:51.942
People are dying in other countries.

00:15:51.942 --> 00:15:53.213
That's why they wanna come here.

00:15:53.213 --> 00:15:54.820
They wanna have lives.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.361
- The fact that we're here just speaks to

00:15:56.361 --> 00:15:58.837
what America can be,
and what it should be.

00:15:58.837 --> 00:16:03.004
- And that we all are just
a human race, you know.

00:16:04.824 --> 00:16:07.407
(bright music)

